# @req REQ-MEM-GUARD-BACKEND-PARITY-001
"""
# `guard` Capability-Matrix Row vs Observed Guard-Page Behaviour

## Overview

`mem_infra_matrix()` in `src/lib/common/mem_infra/config.spl` advertises, per
backend, whether `--mem-infra=guard` gives you guard pages. A capability matrix
is a safety claim: someone reads `guard: true`, enables it to hunt a
use-after-free, and trusts a clean run to mean the UAF is not there.

Until 2026-08-02 that row read `interpreter: true, cranelift: true, llvm: true`
and two of the three were false. The guard-page allocator exists only in
`src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/mem_guard.rs`; cranelift and
llvm both resolve `rt_alloc` to the C runtime, which has no `mmap`/`mprotect`
guard mechanism at all.

This spec exists so that row can never drift back to a claim nobody measured.
It does not read source, count `grep` hits, or check that an env var is
consumed — any of those can be true while the protection does nothing. It
commits a **real use-after-free** in a child process and observes which side of
the fault the process reaches:

| outcome | meaning |
|---------|---------|
| child prints `uaf_probe: survived` and exits 0 | the write landed on ordinary freed heap — **no guard page** |
| child dies on SIGSEGV, never prints `survived` | the write hit a `PROT_NONE` page — **guard page is real** |

Each backend is then asserted to match its own matrix row, so the matrix and
the runtime cannot disagree without this spec going red.

## Sabotage control

Every backend is run twice: once with `SIMPLE_MEM_GUARD_RATE=1` and once with
the knob unset. The knob-off run must survive on *every* backend. That is what
makes the interpreter's trap meaningful — it shows the SIGSEGV is caused by the
guard being switched on, not by the fixture being inherently fatal. A spec that
only ran the guard-on case would pass just as happily against a probe that
segfaulted for unrelated reasons.

## Backend coverage

`interpreter` and `cranelift` are exercised here directly. `llvm` is not run in
this spec because it needs a full `native-build` (minutes, not seconds); it was
measured by hand on 2026-08-02 and is recorded in
`doc/08_tracking/bug/mem_infra_guard_row_false_on_native_backends_2026-07-31.md`
— the native binary survived the UAF at `SIMPLE_MEM_GUARD_RATE=1`, and `nm`
showed it binds a plain-`malloc` C `rt_alloc`, the same no-guard implementation
cranelift resolves. Its matrix row is still asserted here as a static check.

## Related Specifications

- test/01_unit/compiler/interp/mem_guard_rate_spec.spl — sampling-count gate (interpreter only)
- test/01_unit/lib/mem_infra/config_spec.spl — matrix/resolution unit contract
- doc/05_design/runtime/memory_analysis/m2_guard_and_harden_design.md
"""

# @tag: unit, runtime, memory, mem-guard, m2

use std.spec.*
use std.common.mem_infra.config.{mem_infra_matrix, mem_infra_row_supported, mem_infra_auto_rows}
use std.io_runtime.process_run

val FIXTURE = "test/fixture/mem_infra/guard_uaf_probe.spl"
val SURVIVED = "uaf_probe: survived"

# The SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY override is expanded by the CHILD SHELL
# (`${SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY:-bin/simple}`) rather than read Simple-side via
# `env_get`. Either spelling works now; this one is kept only because it makes
# the default visible at the point of use.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-08-02). An earlier version of this comment claimed the shell
# form was load-bearing — that "the spec runner does not surface that variable
# to the spec body", so `env_get` returned "" while the shell form worked. That
# diagnosis was wrong, and acting on it would have left every sibling spec
# broken while looking fixed.
#
# The real defect was never about how the override is spelled. `bin/simple test`
# hands the spec to a long-lived helper process (src/app/test_daemon/light_daemon.spl)
# over a request that carries NO environment, and that helper's own environment
# is frozen at whichever invocation first started it. The spec body therefore
# ran under a stale environment — and so did any shell it forked, since the
# shell inherits the daemon's environment too. Measured: with a daemon already
# running, a spec body saw the PREVIOUS run's value through `env_get` AND
# through `$SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY` in a child shell, on the same run. Kill the
# daemon and both spellings immediately read the caller's value.
#
# Fixed in `test_runner_client.spl`: naming any binary-override variable now
# takes the direct lane instead of the daemon lane, so the override reaches the
# spec body whichever way it is read. Full write-up, including why the
# original sabotage check passed for the wrong reason:
# doc/08_tracking/bug/test_daemon_freezes_env_binary_override_dead_2026-08-02.md
fn survived_uaf(engine: text, guard_on: bool) -> bool:
    val gate = if guard_on: "SIMPLE_MEM_GUARD_RATE=1 " else: ""
    # NB: the `{{`/`}}` are escaped braces — a bare `{...}` in a Simple text
    # literal is string interpolation, so an unescaped `${SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY:-…}`
    # is parsed as an interpolation of a variable named `SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY:-…`
    # and fails to compile rather than reaching the shell.
    val cmd = "{gate}SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE={engine} \"${{SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY:-bin/simple}}\" run '{FIXTURE}' 2>&1"
    val (out, _err, _code) = process_run("/bin/sh", ["-c", cmd])
    out.contains(SURVIVED)

describe "guard capability-matrix row matches observed guard-page behaviour":

    it "traps a use-after-free on the interpreter, which claims guard: true":
        step("Confirm the matrix claims guard support on the interpreter")
        assert_equal(mem_infra_row_supported("guard", "interpreter"), true)

        step("Sabotage control: with the guard knob unset the same UAF must survive")
        assert_equal(survived_uaf("interpreter", false), true)

        step("With SIMPLE_MEM_GUARD_RATE=1 the UAF must NOT survive - the guard page traps it")
        assert_equal(survived_uaf("interpreter", true), false)

    it "does NOT trap a use-after-free on cranelift, which claims guard: false":
        step("Confirm the matrix does not claim guard support on cranelift")
        assert_equal(mem_infra_row_supported("guard", "cranelift"), false)

        step("Sabotage control: with the guard knob unset the UAF survives")
        assert_equal(survived_uaf("jit", false), true)

        step("With SIMPLE_MEM_GUARD_RATE=1 the UAF still survives - the knob is inert here")
        assert_equal(survived_uaf("jit", true), true)

    it "claims guard: false on llvm, which links the same no-guard C rt_alloc as cranelift":
        step("Confirm the matrix does not claim guard support on llvm")
        assert_equal(mem_infra_row_supported("guard", "llvm"), false)

    it "never expands auto into guard on a backend that cannot honour it":
        step("auto on the interpreter includes guard - it is genuinely supported there")
        assert_equal(_contains(mem_infra_auto_rows("interpreter"), "guard"), true)

        step("auto on cranelift and llvm must omit guard rather than silently claim it")
        assert_equal(_contains(mem_infra_auto_rows("cranelift"), "guard"), false)
        assert_equal(_contains(mem_infra_auto_rows("llvm"), "guard"), false)

fn _contains(items: [text], value: text) -> bool:
    for item in items:
        if item == value:
            return true
    false
